CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure illustrates the concept of vanishing points arising when a static camera observes an object translating at constant velocity. A bird flies away from camera center O along a straight receding path, depicted as a sequence of silhouettes with equal-length red velocity arrows labeled V (the bird's constant 3D velocity); it is the projected image-plane velocity arrows that grow shorter as the bird recedes. Green projection rays from O through the image plane to each successive bird position show that as the bird recedes, its image on the plane converges toward a single point labeled Vanishing point, and the image-plane velocity arrows shrink and all point toward that same location. The lower portion of the figure shows the corresponding image-plane view, where the bird projected positions collapse toward the vanishing point and the image velocity approaches zero. This figure demonstrates that for pure constant-velocity translation, the image positions and velocities converge toward the vanishing point of the motion direction — the focus of expansion — whose location encodes the direction of translation.

PROJECTION TYPE: perspective
Use the scaffold's true perspective projection after building all geometry. Use this only when the source has real vanishing-point convergence or distance-based size shrinkage; then depth should visibly affect apparent size.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Camera center O: solid black sphere at the origin, labeled 'O' below it
  - Image plane: a thin dark-gray outlined rectangle (drawn as a tilted parallelogram) standing in front of O at focal distance f, its lower-left corner region sitting just behind/right of O
  - Bird silhouette in 3D: white bird outline with black stroke, shown at 7 successive positions along a straight receding path from lower-left to upper-right
  - Red 3D velocity arrows: thick equal-length red arrows labeled V drawn between consecutive 3D bird positions (constant-magnitude 3D velocity), including one entering from the left of frame and one exiting right
  - Bold label 'V' above the red 3D arrow(s)
  - Green projection rays: straight green lines from O through the image plane out to each 3D bird position; rays for the nearest/earliest positions drawn faded/translucent
  - Projected bird images on the image plane: small white bird silhouettes, progressively smaller and crowding toward the vanishing point
  - Thin red image-velocity arrows on the image plane connecting consecutive projected bird images, shrinking in length as the bird recedes
  - Vanishing point: small solid black dot on the image plane where the ray from O parallel to V pierces the plane, labeled 'Vanishing point'
  - Green direction ray from O parallel to the 3D velocity V, passing exactly through the vanishing point and continuing far to the right, annotated with a thick red arrow and bold label 'V'
  - Optional faint principal axis / plane border ticks for orientation

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World units: camera center O at origin (black sphere r≈0.09). Optical axis is +Z (receding to screen-right/away); +X screen-right, +Y screen-up. Image plane is the plane Z = f with f = 2.5, drawn as a rectangle spanning X∈[-1.5,1.8], Y∈[-1.2,1.5] (3.3 × 2.7 units), outlined in dark gray #333333 at ~3px, no fill (or 5% gray fill). In the source view this rectangle reads as a tall parallelogram whose lower-left corner sits just right of and behind O, with the plane receding to the right — keep O visually outside/left-below the plane's lower-left corner. Bird path: P(t) = P0 + V·t with P0 = (-1.0, 1.2, 3.0) and V = (1.2, 0.35, 2.2) units per unit time; slider t∈[0,1] maps to t_world∈[0,7]. Draw 7 sampled 3D birds at t_world = 0,1,...,6 (bird silhouette ≈0.55 units wide, white fill #FFFFFF with 3px black outline, wings-up 'M' shape as in source, oriented facing +X). Thick red 3D velocity arrows #EE0000 (shaft ~0.10 units thick) between consecutive 3D birds, all exactly equal length, plus one arrow entering at the far left and one exiting at the far right; bold black label 'V' above one of the middle arrows. Projection: x = f·X/Z, y = f·Y/Z. Vanishing point = f·(Vx/Vz, Vy/Vz) = (1.364, 0.398, 2.5) — a black dot r≈0.05 with the serif label 'Vanishing point' placed below-right of it. Projected bird images: same silhouette scaled by f/Z (so ~0.46 units at t=0 down to ~0.11 units at t=6), converging from upper-left (≈(-0.83, 1.00)) toward the vanishing point. Thin red arrows (#EE0000, ~1.5px) chain consecutive projected images, each visibly shorter than the last. Green rays #00CC00 from O to each 3D bird, 2px; the two or three earliest/nearest rays drawn at 25-45% opacity to match the faded rays in the source. One extra green ray from O along direction V continues well past the plane to the right edge of the frame, passing exactly through the vanishing point, annotated mid-length with a thick red arrow and bold 'V'. Palette: green #00CC00, red #EE0000, plane/outlines #333333, text black on white background.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The green rays, image plane and bird path form one 3D pencil of lines through O; rotating shows the projected birds really lie on the plane where each ray pierces it."
  },
  {
    "id": "flightTime",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Bird position along path (time t)",
    "range": [
      0,
      1,
      0.005
    ],
    "default": 0,
    "teaches": "Equal steps of 3D motion produce ever-smaller steps on the image plane: image velocity falls off as the bird recedes even though V is constant."
  },
  {
    "id": "flyLoop",
    "type": "animation",
    "label": "Fly (constant V)",
    "loop": true,
    "teaches": "The 3D motion is uniform, yet the projected bird decelerates and creeps toward the vanishing point — the image velocity tends to zero."
  },
  {
    "id": "dragVelocity",
    "type": "drag",
    "teaches": "Dragging the 3D velocity direction relocates the vanishing point to the image of the new direction — the focus of expansion encodes the translation direction, not the speed."
  },
  {
    "id": "viewMode",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "View",
    "options": [
      "3D scene",
      "Camera image plane"
    ],
    "default": "3D scene",
    "teaches": "Seen face-on, the projected bird track is a straight line collapsing onto the vanishing point with shrinking velocity arrows — exactly what a camera records."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Bird near the camera",
    "narration": "The bird starts close and to the left. Its projection lands far from the vanishing point, and one step of 3D motion sweeps a long arrow across the image plane.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "3D scene",
      "flightTime": 0.05
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Same V, smaller image motion",
    "narration": "The red 3D arrows stay equal in length — the bird never changes speed — but the projected bird's steps on the image plane are already noticeably shorter.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "3D scene",
      "flightTime": 0.45
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Converging on the vanishing point",
    "narration": "Far away, successive projections crowd together next to the black dot. The image velocity approaches zero at the vanishing point of the motion direction.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "3D scene",
      "flightTime": 0.95
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "What the camera sees",
    "narration": "Switching to the image plane, the whole sequence is a straight 2D track with arrows shrinking toward the focus of expansion — the point that reveals which way the bird is translating.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "Camera image plane",
      "flightTime": 0.95
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "azimuthDeg": 35,
  "elevationDeg": 18,
  "rollDeg": 0,
  "heightFraction": 0.62
}

NOTES: Build the bird as a flat extruded/plane-mapped silhouette (THREE.Shape or a texture on a PlaneGeometry) that always billboards to face the camera in the 3D scene, and that is rendered coplanar with the image plane for the projected copies (offset +0.005 in Z to avoid z-fighting). Use Line2/LineMaterial (fat lines) for the green rays and plane outline so they hold constant screen width. Red arrows: cylinder shaft + cone head, or ArrowHelper with custom head size; rebuild lengths each frame from the projection formula rather than baking them. Labels 'O', 'V', 'Vanishing point' as CSS2DObject HTML so they stay crisp and upright under orbit. Slider and animation share one time state variable; pressing play resumes from the slider value and loops t 0→1 over ~6 s with easing off. Drag interaction: a draggable handle on the far end of the V direction ray constrained to a sphere around O; on drag, recompute V, the vanishing point, all 7 bird positions, rays, projections and arrow lengths in one update pass. Toggle to 'Camera image plane' animates the camera to a front-on view along -Z at the plane (or swaps to an orthographic camera framing the plane rectangle) and hides the 3D birds/rays except faint ghosts.

Follow the interaction plan above.

